\title[Graded Lawvere Theory]{Graded Lawvere Theory:\\ Obstruction Spectra for
Self-Applicative Presentations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{self-applicative presentation} consists of a set $A$ of codes, a set $B$
of meanings, and an evaluation table $\ev : A \times A \to B$ permitting a code
to be applied to a code, in particular to itself; it is \emph{universal} when
every observation $g : A \to B$ is performed by some code. Universality is the
abstract form of the ``sufficiently rich system'' hypothesis behind the
G\"odel, Turing, Tarski and Lawvere diagonal arguments. We prove that this
hypothesis is \emph{vacuous}: a universal presentation over $B$ exists only when
$B$ is a subsingleton, since every domain with two distinct meanings carries a
uniformly definable fixed-point-free endomorphism. We then develop
\emph{graded universality}, requiring only representability of the single
diagonal composite $x \mapsto f(\ev(x,x))$. The diagonal argument survives, and
admits a complete converse: for any family $F$ of endomorphisms of $B$, a single
table diagonally represents every member of $F$ if and only if every member has
a fixed point. Representability is therefore a pointwise semantic invariant;
in particular it is \emph{not} controlled by the transformation monoid generated
by $F$, as two involutions on a three-element domain generating a
fixed-point-free three-cycle show. We further prove that uniqueness of semantic
fixed points forces all diagonal codes, even in unrelated presentations, to
share a value (with contractions on $\mathbb{R}$ as a metric instance); that
transport across presentations degrades through a strict four-level hierarchy
(equivalence, coherent retraction, embedding, bisimulation) with separating
counterexamples; that a nonvacuous formal/visual/contrapuntal triad over the
pitch classes $\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z}$ exists with common diagonal value $0$
for inversion while semitone transposition is representable in no syntax at all;
and that over an $n$-element domain exactly $n^n - (n-1)^n$ endomorphisms are
representable, with asymptotic density $1 - e^{-1} \approx 0.6321$.
\end{abstract}

