\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic density]
\[
  \frac{n^n - (n-1)^n}{n^n} = 1 - \Bigl(1-\tfrac1n\Bigr)^n
  \;\longrightarrow\; 1 - e^{-1} = 0.632120\ldots
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\bigl(1+\tfrac{-1}{n}\bigr)^n \to e^{-1}$; subtract from $1$ and identify
$\bigl(\tfrac{n-1}{n}\bigr)^n$ with $\bigl(1+\tfrac{-1}{n}\bigr)^n$ for
$n \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

A fixed proportion $e^{-1} \approx 36.8\%$ of finite semantic dynamics is
permanently outside self-applicative syntax, in any medium and at any scale.

\section{Discussion}

The classical picture holds that self-reference arises once a system is
sufficiently rich. Theorem~\ref{thm:vacuity} shows that the strongest reading of
richness is not demanding but inconsistent over nontrivial semantics; what
survives, and what the classical arguments actually use, is representability of
a single diagonal composite, and Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} shows this is
\emph{equivalent} to a purely semantic condition. Capacity for self-reference is
therefore not a property of a syntax but of the semantic dynamics the syntax is
asked to internalise.

Theorem~\ref{thm:monoid} is the sharpest structural finding: it rules out an
entire family of plausible reformulations (orbit structure, generated monoid,
group-theoretic invariants) in favour of the pointwise predicate. The reason is
a resource fact---the diagonal argument applies $f$ once---suggesting the general
principle that the strength of a diagonalisation is bounded by how often it
consumes the semantic operator.

Section~\ref{sec:choice} shows that presentation ambiguity is canonical while
code ambiguity is genuine and collapses exactly under semantic uniqueness;
Section~\ref{sec:counting} gives the subject a quantitative face, answering
``how much self-reference is possible?'' with $1-e^{-1}$.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \emph{Iterated self-application.} Extend diagonal representation to
  depth $k$, requiring a table to represent $x \mapsto f(\ev(x,\ev(x,x)))$ and
  its higher analogues. Conjecture: depth-$k$ representability holds iff every
  member of the family has a point of period dividing $k$ under a suitable
  auxiliary transformation, stratifying the spectrum into a strictly increasing
  chain measurable against $n^n-(n-1)^n$, strict for all $n \ge 3$.
  \item \emph{Density laws as invariants.} For finite semantic domains with
  extra structure (monoid actions, partial orders, metric quantisations),
  conjecture that the representable density converges to a structure-dependent
  constant that separates the structures: monotone self-maps of a finite chain
  have density $1$, permutations $1-e^{-1}+o(1)$.
  \item \emph{Completeness of the coherence hierarchy.} Conjecture that
  equivalence, coherent retraction, embedding and bisimulation are the only four
  levels, with the associated preservation laws exhaustive.
  \item \emph{Bounded-complexity fragments.} Grade representability by the
  complexity of the observation represented, and determine the minimal closure
  axioms under which diagonal fixed points persist.
  \item \emph{Beyond sets.} Lift the development to categories with finite
  products, partial maps, or enriched hom-objects, with the free graded model as
  the universal witness.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Full universality is impossible over any semantics with two distinct meanings,
and the obstruction is uniform rather than Boolean-specific. Replacing it by
representability of a single diagonal composite recovers the whole diagonal
mechanism and admits an exact converse: a family of semantic endomorphisms is
jointly representable precisely when each member has a fixed point. This
invariant is pointwise, not monoidal and not cardinal; the diagonal value is
canonical exactly under semantic uniqueness; transport degrades in four
controlled stages; a nonvacuous formal/visual/contrapuntal triad over the twelve
pitch classes exists with common diagonal value $0$; and the representable share
of an $n$-element domain is $n^n-(n-1)^n$, tending to $1-e^{-1}$.

\end{document}

